; K1 paw pad, rev A: a stick-on rubber skin for the bare-paw squeeze grasp
; (ipse docs/prereg/2026-11-bare-paw.md, Amendment 4; in sim as PAD_CAD=1, D3b).
;
; WHY: two bare R25 paw bulbs pinch a can with two point contacts. Those can't
; resist the can turning about the pinch line, and in phyz the can tilted out
; 0 -> 23 deg during every lift. A flat rubber face gives a contact patch that
; carries a friction couple, so the can holds.
;
; GEOMETRY: the inner face is a sphere conforming to the bulb (R25 + 0.25 mm
; glue gap); the outer face is FLAT, 30 x 30 mm, 3 mm proud of the bulb at its
; centre. That flat face is the whole point: a curved outer face is a point
; contact again.
; Frame: bulb centre at the origin, forearm axis = Y, pad faces +Z (toward the
; can). Mount with the face pointing at the opposite paw at the grasp pose:
; on the K1's hand link, the direction from the bulb centre to the can axis
; that the sim computes from the grasp IK (bare_paw_session, PADS).
;
; MATERIAL: cast Shore-A 40-60 silicone, or nitrile sheet over a printed TPU
; 95A core, glued with silicone adhesive. The sim assumes mu 0.95. Printed TPU
; alone is closer to mu 0.6-0.8, which is untested in sim.
; KNOWN MISMATCH: the sim pad is 40 mm along the forearm x 30 mm. The bulb is
; only 30 mm long in Y (Right_Arm_4.STL, y 198-228 mm), so this part is
; 30 x 30. D3b re-runs the held-out pick with this exact pad.
; Volume check (vcad STL export): 5608 mm^3, z 14.0 -> 28.25 mm.
[defparam bulb_r 25.0]
[defparam glue_gap 0.25]
[defparam face_t 3.0]
[defparam face_w 30.0]
[material "silicone" 0.15 0.15 0.15 0.0 0.9]
[let inner-r [+ 25.0 0.25]]
[let block [translate -15.0 -15.0 14.0 [cube 30.0 30.0 14.25]]]
[let pad [difference [sphere-n inner-r 96] block]]
[root pad "silicone"]
